        # ReleaseNotes of this module
        ReleaseNotes = 'v0.10.15: bash-semantics parity batch. Arithmetic: $(( )) and (( )) now evaluate with faithful bash integer C-operator semantics (real Int64 evaluator) — **, integer /, bitwise/shift, 1/0 comparisons, ternary, and number bases — replacing the verbatim-PowerShell approximation. printf: %c prints the first char of its arg, %f honors width/zero-pad, and printf no longer drops its own trailing newline (printf "%s\n" b; echo c now prints b/c on two lines, was "bc"). Arrays/expansion: ${!arr[@]} yields indices for indexed arrays and keys for associative ones (and no longer crashes on an EMPTY array — the bare-arg form emits @(...) which unrolls empty to zero args instead of binding a spurious $null), quoted "${arr[@]}" iterates per-element in for-in, ${var%pat} uses shortest-suffix removal, and negative/space slice offsets are handled. read -ra and IFS are honored; a NAME=VAL env prefix now applies to mapped commands. test / [ ]: bare string-literal operands are quoted so they compare correctly. xargs splits default input on any whitespace like GNU. The launcher<->host connection flow is hardened against mid-command failures. v0.10.14: coreutils flag-coverage expansion + review fixes. Full gzip/tar flag support within .NET limits: gzip implements -r/-q/-S SUF/--fast/--best and accept-ignores -n/-N/-a/--rsyncable/--synchronous/-L (no GZipStream header mapping); tar implements -k/--keep-old-files and -a/--auto-compress (gzip-by-extension) and refuses bzip2/xz/zstd/compress (no managed .NET codec) even inside a bundle (-cjf no longer silently writes an uncompressed tar). The unsupported-flag classifier now also covers find (an unknown predicate errors instead of being read as a search path) and the file movers cp/mv/rm/mkdir/rmdir (valid-but-unsupported flags get a clear refusal; -- ends flag parsing). tee --append now appends (was wrongly refused). Low-hanging-fruit flags: long-form aliases of already-working short flags wired for cat (--number/--number-nonblank/--squeeze-blank/--show-ends/--show-tabs), wc (--lines/--words/--bytes), cut (--fields/--delimiter/--characters), head/tail (--lines/--bytes), and sort (--reverse/--numeric-sort/--unique/--ignore-case/--key/--field-separator/--human-numeric-sort/--version-sort/--month-sort/--check/--dictionary-order/--ignore-leading-blanks/--stable/--sort=WORD); plus sort -g/--general-numeric-sort, du --exclude=GLOB, base64 -i/--ignore-garbage, comm --total, expand -i/--first-only, and head/tail -q. Review fixes: gzip now refuses to re-compress a file already ending in the suffix (gzip -r over a tree with *.gz no longer creates *.gz.gz and deletes the original); the mover classifier no longer skips a bad flag placed before --. v0.10.13: tar / gzip / sed / jq engine hardening (GNU/jq audit, all with regression tests). Security: tar extract refuses path-traversal (tar-slip / Zip-Slip) — escaping members (../x, absolute, drive/UNC) and escaping symlink/hardlink targets are no longer written outside the destination. tar --exclude is now an fnmatch glob (was a substring test; an empty --exclude= no longer drops everything); tar extract handles symlink/hardlink/directory entries; gzip -l size is 64-bit (no >2GB overflow); gzip force-deletes a read-only source. sed gains the numeric occurrence flag (s/x/y/2, /2g), the = and Q commands, address negation (2!d, /re/!cmd), step addresses (1~2), N,/re/ ranges (incl. 0,/re/), and the s///p flag; \n \t \r expand in replacements and \& is a literal ampersand (bare & is the whole match). jq: the // alternative streams every truthy value (was first-only); arithmetic needs no surrounding spaces (1+2, .foo-1, "a"+"b"); object construction is a Cartesian product over multi-valued fields ({a:(1,2)} streams two objects); computed keys {(expr):v} are evaluated; from_entries preserves insertion order. v0.10.12: Per-session host daemon (multi-agent contention fix) + a Tab-completion fix. The ps-bash-host daemon is now keyed per (user, session) instead of one shared daemon per user. Independent shells / agents each get their own warm daemon — the session token is the PSBASH_SESSION env var, or when unset the launcher''s parent process id — so N concurrent callers no longer pile onto a single host and starve its warm runspace pool. That contention was the cause of multi-second-to-multi-minute stalls when many ps-bash -c invocations ran at once (e.g. several Claude Code agents sharing one machine, each command discarding its runspace and forcing the shared pool to cold-warm under CPU saturation). Repeated invocations from one shell still share a hot daemon (warm reuse ~0.2s); only independent sessions diverge, and each session''s daemon is reaped by idle-shutdown. When no session token is available the historical per-user endpoint is used (back-compat). Tab completion: a command-position token containing a slash or ~ (./script, ../bin/x, dir/x, ~/x) now does filename completion over directories AND files — so ./sc<Tab> offers ./scripts/ — matching bash, instead of returning nothing (it previously scanned only files in the cwd and never directories). v0.10.11: jq operators and the v0.10.10 publish fix. jq gains the non-arithmetic operator set — sort, unique, reverse, min, max, first, last, flatten, sort_by(e), unique_by(e), group_by(e), to_entries, from_entries, has(k), @csv/@tsv/@base64/@base64d/@json, and string builtins join/split/startswith/endswith/ltrimstr/rtrimstr — plus binary arithmetic (+ - * / %) with jq type overloads and boolean and/or. v0.10.10 binaries failed to publish because a Pester test still asserted printf %s "first arg only"; printf now correctly recycles its format over multiple args (bash semantics), and the test was corrected. v0.10.10: Command flag-coverage sweep (prioritized by utility and complexity). Correctness fixes for wrong/destructive behavior: kill -0 was killing the process (now an existence probe); cut open ranges (-f2-) threw; env NAME=VAL cmd (run-a-command-with-modified-environment) was unimplemented; cp -p leaked as a filename operand; paste -d emitted a literal backslash-n; md5sum -b was treated as a filename; realpath -e was a no-op. New flag support across ~25 commands: cut -s/--output-delimiter; cp -a/-u; wc -m/-L; stat --format; touch -r; du --max-depth; tree --noreport/-f; sort -o; nl -w/-s/-v/-i/-n/-b; uniq -D; grep -f/--exclude-from; find -type l/-print; date format codes (%I %R %D %z %u ...) and -d @EPOCH; printf format recycling and %i/%u/%e/%g; ls --group-directories-first; rg -S/-s/-x (smart-case/line-regexp); jq add/tostring/tonumber/ascii_downcase/ascii_upcase; tar --strip-components/-O; gzip -t; md5sum/sha* -c (verify); join -a/-v/-j/-i; split -b/--additional-suffix; xargs -d. Each comes with regression tests. Still pending: jq operators (group_by, @csv, arithmetic) and awk control flow (if/for/while) are engine-level projects. v0.10.9: Interactive shell features and runtime bug fixes. (1) cd - returns to the previous directory ($OLDPWD) and echoes it like bash; every successful cd now records $OLDPWD, and cd - with no prior directory reports "cd: OLDPWD not set" instead of failing on an empty path. (2) Bash history expansion in the interactive shell: !! (previous command), !n / !-n (by session index), !str / !?str? (prefix / substring search), the !$ / !^ / !* word designators and :n suffixes, and ^old^new quick substitution; expansion is suppressed inside single quotes and after a backslash, leaving the ! negation operator untouched. (3) ln -sf no longer destroys a populated directory tree: when the link name is a real directory the link is created inside it (basename of the target) and -f only removes a file or symlink, matching GNU ln (the old code ran a recursive delete). (4) awk file-operand mode: awk ''{...}'' file.txt now reads the named file(s) instead of silently dropping the operand and reading nothing. (5) kill fixes: named-signal short forms (kill -KILL / -TERM / -SIGTERM pid) are honored, and the pid loop no longer collides with the read-only $PID automatic variable, which silently broke every kill. (6) Stale-host fix: the daemon build identity now folds the host binary file stamp, so a recompiled host (same version, new code) replaces a running daemon instead of being silently reused. (7) Docs: new Interactive Shell guide and a Windows coreutils (uutils) comparison. v0.10.8: Robustness and interactive performance. (1) Interactive typing latency: keystrokes echo before the history/flag prediction runs, and prediction is fully async/debounced so a busy or IO-bound system never stalls input (set PSBASH_NO_PREDICT to disable it); the prompt is now drawn before the runspace finishes starting (startup type-ahead). (2) Hash-based transpile cache: startup .sh scripts transpile once and are cached on disk by content hash, with an in-memory LRU for repeated longer commands. (3) Bash-tool dogfood fixes: if/while/until conditions now test the command EXIT CODE rather than its output, so "if grep -q ..." and "if ! cmd" branch correctly; [ ... ] / test are silent (no stray True/False on stdout); every command resets the exit code on success (a trailing pwd no longer reports a stale 127); piping into "while read" now feeds the loop body; /dev/null as a file operand is an empty file instead of a crash; "cp -rf" bundled short flags are honored; and rm -rf / cp / mv / find -delete clear the read-only attribute so they no longer fail partway on Windows .git and node_modules trees. (4) grep gained --include / --exclude / --exclude-dir, -L/--files-without-match, -x/--line-regexp, -s/--no-messages, and -P/--perl-regexp. (5) "<cmd> --version" now identifies ps-bash across all commands. (6) Internals: emitted PowerShell is built through one shared builder and destructive filesystem operations through shared OS helpers, eliminating a class of one-off seam bugs (e.g. a negated-condition that took the wrong branch). Full version history: https://github.com/standardbeagle/ps-bash/releases'

    } # End of PSData hashtable

